  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The last human moon landing was Apollo 17, which landed on the Moon on December 11, 1972. NASA astronauts Eugene Cernan and Harrison Schmitt spent over three days exploring the Taurus-Littrow valley, marking the final time humans have walked on the lunar surface to date. Key details of the last moon landing: Mission: Apollo 17 (NASA's final Apollo mission). Location: Taurus-Littrow Valley. Last People on the Moon: Eugene Cernan (Commander) and Harrison Schmitt (Lunar Module Pilot). Last Man on the Moon: Eugene Cernan was the last to leave the lunar surface on December 14, 1972. Duration: The crew spent approximately 73 hours on the surface, completing three successful, long-duration excursions. This video shows footage of the last humans walking on the moon:25sScience ExplainedYouTube• Oct 27, 2024 25s Apollo 17 was notable for being the first mission to include a trained scientist (geologist Harrison Schmitt) and for collecting the most samples and performing the most experiments of any Apollo mission. While NASA's Apollo program ended with this mission, it remains the last human visit to the Moon until the upcoming Artemis missions.",
